Local patch for 3rd/cproc (michaelforney/cproc at d1c53dd), applied after cproc-empty-copy.patch to the build
copy by scripts/setup-qbe.sh.

A qualifier on an array type (`const S` for a typedef `S` of an array) belongs to the array's elements (C11
6.7.3p9). cproc kept it on the array node, so `const S *` (the pointer's qualifier, taken from the array node)
did not match `const unsigned char (*)[12]` (the same array with its elements qualified): cproc reported "base
types of pointer assignment must be compatible or void" on EDG's generated C (ifc_map_functions_acc and
ifc_map_functions_val: `copy_from_node_field(..., universal.get_storage(), ...)`). gcc accepts it.

The fix moves a declarator's base qualifier onto the element array when the base is an array (decl.c, qualarray),
and compares a pointer's pointee qualifiers with the array's element qualifiers (expr.c, pointeequal), so an
assignment that drops a qualifier is still rejected (`unsigned char (*p)[12] = <const S *>` gives "assignment to
pointer discards qualifiers", checked by hand). tests/c/array_qual.c is the regression test (it fails with the
unpatched cproc at the same construct).
--- a/decl.c
+++ b/decl.c
@@ -691,6 +691,21 @@
 	}
